  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatApp3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "Your doctor explains that the trial treatment you received has now been
    approved for wider use. Even though approval has been granted, your doctor explains
    that they still carefully track how the treatment performs in real-world patients
    like you.\r\n\r\n<b>Why do researchers continue to monitor treatments after they\u2019ve
    been approved?</b>\r\n"
  explanation: 'Even after approval, ongoing safety monitoring (AKA: post-market
    surveillance) helps ensure new treatments remain safe and effective in the real
    world, especially once they are used by larger, more diverse groups. This allows
    researchers, doctors, and regulatory agencies like the Food and Drug Administration
    (FDA) to track outcomes and monitor any new side effects or long-term effects
    that may appear, helping to protect patients and improve how treatments are used
    over time.'
  correctAnswer: To watch for rare or long-term side effects that may not have shown
    up during the trial
  incorrectAnswer1: To decide which patients should be the first to receive the newly
    approved treatment
  incorrectAnswer2: To determine the appropriate cost of the newly approved treatment
  incorrectAnswer3: To test whether patient participants followed the rules of the
    trial
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Approval
